<p><code>x = e</code> declares an immutable variable. <code>var x: T = e</code> and <code>x: T := e</code> declare a mutable one, and <code>x := e</code> assigns to it (<code>Specification/basic/variables.tex</code>). These are Scala's <code>val</code> and <code>var</code>, or Swift's <code>let</code> and <code>var</code>. A field is immutable unless it is declared with <code>var</code>. A <span class="cut"><code>value</code></span> <span class="add">value object is a tuple, <code>()</code>, or an</span> object <span class="add">whose declaration, or a trait that it extends, has the <code>value</code> modifier, as the library's numbers, characters and booleans do. It</span> has only immutable fields and value semantics, like a Swift <code>struct</code>. <span class="add">Any other object, except a function, is a reference object, like an instance of a Swift class. Each construction makes a new reference object, distinct from all others (<code>Specification/basic/objects.tex:24-33</code>).</span></p>
