<p><strong>Why, 8 November.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of the rule's meaning: the overlap stays, and the paragraph now gives each fix in full. Your question on this change, at 00:22 UTC on 8 November, on how the rule relates to the POPL 2019 paper on variance, raised it. The research for the answer found that the paper's own calculus has one fix: an object, which excludes every trait that it does not extend. A run showed that walk loads and runs this form. At 00:49 UTC the coordinator said that this change would name it. The text now gives it as a third fix, with its cost: no other type can extend an object. Its first wording said &ldquo;declare the type as an object&rdquo;. The cold read found that a worker could take this as met by the trait's extenders, which are often objects already, while the <code>+</code> stays in the trait. It now says to declare the <code>+</code> in an object, not in a trait. The cold read also found that the paragraph told a worker to make the trait exclude arrays, but named no trait, so the worker had to search the library. It now names it: <code>excludes { HasRank }</code>, the trait that the library's arrays extend. And since an object is not an exclusion, the last sentence gives <code>-</code>, <code>MIN</code> and <code>MAX</code> &ldquo;the same fix&rdquo;, in place of &ldquo;the same exclusion&rdquo;.</p></div>
