<section id="l39" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 39 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;</span>A type without its own = compares by strict equivalence</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what came in; nothing went</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, lines 55 to 62, a new rule after the rule on pairs and triples</p>
<p><span class="add"><strong>Where a type declares no <code>=</code>, <code>=</code> is strict equivalence, <code>SEQV</code> (P-168).</strong> Strict equivalence is the operator <code>&#x2263;</code> (STRICTLY EQUIVALENT TO), written <code>SEQV</code> or <code>===</code> in ASCII. The operator <code>&#x2261;</code> (IDENTICAL TO, <code>EQV</code> or <code>EQUIV</code>) is a different one: logical equivalence on booleans. The interpreter's library declares it for no type, and walk stops on it (row 184): write <code>a &lt;-&gt; b</code>. The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> answers <code>a SEQV b</code>. <code>SEQV</code> follows the specification on both paths:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span class="add">values of different types are never equivalent;</span></li>
<li><span class="add">a value object is equivalent field by field, a tuple element by element, an integer, a character or a boolean by its value, and a float by its bits;</span></li>
<li><span class="add">a reference object, a string among them, is equivalent only to itself;</span></li>
<li><span class="add">a function is equivalent to itself, but may be found unequal to another that behaves the same.</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span class="add">So for a value object <code>V</code> without its own <code>=</code>, <code>V(s) = V(t)</code> is <code>false</code> where <code>s</code> and <code>t</code> are equal strings made by two computations. Under walk, each evaluation of a string literal makes a new string, unlike Java, which interns literals. So <code>"ab" SEQV "ab"</code> and <code>V("ab") = V("ab")</code> are <code>false</code>. The library declares <code>=</code> on pairs and triples only, so <code>(s, 1, 2, 3) = (t, 1, 2, 3)</code> is <code>false</code> too. Both <code>false</code> answers are the rule's: assert them as correct. Under walk a float numeral is a <code>FloatLiteral</code>, a type of its own, so it is not equivalent to a computed <code>RR64</code> (<code>interpreter.md</code>, &ldquo;How walk evaluates a program&rdquo;). To compare by content, give the value object an <code>=</code>, or compare the parts. A string's <code>=</code> compares text. Under walk, <code>&#x2260;</code> (<code>=/=</code> or <code>NE</code>) and <code>assert(x, y, msg)</code> reach a type's own <code>=</code> only if the type extends a library trait that declares <code>=</code>, such as <code>Equality</code> (row 807). Otherwise write <code>assert(x = y, msg)</code>. Until the switch-over, the compiler's library declares no <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code> and none on tuples. Its own <code>&#x2263;</code>, declared as <code>===</code>, compares strings and <code>ZZ32</code>, <code>ZZ64</code>, <code>RR32</code> and <code>RR64</code> by <code>=</code>, not by bits or identity (row 908).</span></p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> A new rule. The batch made both paths answer <code>SEQV</code>, strict equivalence, by the specification's rule, as your decision on its meaning asks. The library's <code>=</code> over <code>Any</code>, which every type without its own <code>=</code> falls back to, answers <code>SEQV</code>. The old part said nothing of this. A worker would expect such an <code>=</code> to compare content, and would take the two <code>false</code> answers in the new text for defects. The new rule says how <code>SEQV</code> compares, gives those two cases and says to assert them as correct, and gives the fix. It also names walk's limit on <code>assert</code>, and what the compiler's library does until the switch-over. After the cold read, it says how an integer, a character or a boolean compares: by its value. It names a string's kind as &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo;, in place of &ldquo;a reference object&rdquo;, which the skill does not define. It writes the two <code>false</code> cases as examples, in place of a string &ldquo;built apart&rdquo;, which a worker could not apply. It points to walk's float numerals (change 42). And it says what <code>===</code> is: the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Why, 8 November.</strong> Neither a new rule nor a change of the rule's meaning: what <code>SEQV</code> answers stays as the batch made it. The text now spells the operators out, and it adds three cases where a worker would guess wrong. Your comments on this change raised most of it. At 00:35 UTC on 8 November you asked to check the Unicode spelling of <code>SEQV</code> and <code>=/=</code>, and at 00:58 you asked that the text give both spellings and tell &ldquo;strictly equivalent to&rdquo; from &ldquo;identical to&rdquo;. The batch's text called <code>===</code> &ldquo;the ASCII spelling of <code>SEQV</code>&rdquo;, but <code>SEQV</code> is an ASCII spelling too, and the text never showed the characters. It now gives all three spellings of strict equivalence, <code>&#x2263;</code>, <code>SEQV</code> and <code>===</code>; it says that <code>&#x2261;</code> is another operator; and it says that <code>=/=</code> is <code>&#x2260;</code>. At 00:44 you asked that the skill use &ldquo;reference object&rdquo;, the term of the specification and of peer languages such as Swift. The batch's cold read had put &ldquo;an object not declared <code>value</code>&rdquo; in its place, since the skill did not define the term. Change 49 now defines it, so the bullet on strings says &ldquo;a reference object&rdquo;. A first wording of the fix called <code>&#x2261;</code> &ldquo;if and only if&rdquo; on booleans. The cold read ran it under walk, and walk stops on it, since the interpreter's library declares <code>&#x2261;</code> for no type. The text now calls it logical equivalence, the specification's words, and says to write <code>a &lt;-&gt; b</code>. The cold read also found two cases that the text left to a guess. Under walk, two equal string literals are two strings, so <code>"ab" SEQV "ab"</code> is <code>false</code>, where a worker who knows Java expects one shared string. And once change 49 took functions out of the reference objects, no bullet said what <code>SEQV</code> gives on two functions. A new bullet says it, from the specification.</p></div>
</section>

